 ## Overview
-Voxel Grid Storage and Voxelization is the process of converting continuous geometric representations (such as meshes or implicit shapes) into a discrete, dense 3D grid of scalar values or occupancy flags [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of spatial discretization required for volume rendering, collision detection, and mesh processing by mapping world-space coordinates to array indices [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Voxel Grid Construction](3085-voxel-grid-construction.md) by focusing on the fundamental storage layout and the core voxelization algorithm (SDF evaluation or ray casting) rather than higher-level construction pipelines, and from [Instanced Voxel Rendering](10592-instanced-voxel-rendering.md) by providing the raw data structure rather than the rendering optimization [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+Voxel Grid Storage and Voxelization is the process of converting continuous geometric representations (such as meshes or signed distance fields) into a discrete, dense 3D grid of scalar or boolean values [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of representing arbitrary 3D shapes in a format suitable for ray marching, marching cubes, or GPU-based rendering by mapping world-space coordinates to array indices [S1, S3, unofficial]. It differs from sparse representations (like [Adaptive Voxel Hashing Refined by Depth Residual](17590-adaptive-voxel-hashing-refined-by-depth-residual.md)) by allocating memory for the entire bounding volume, and from [Instanced Voxel Rendering](10592-instanced-voxel-rendering.md) by focusing on the data structure and conversion logic rather than the rendering pipeline [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
